  <h4><strong>Think about:</strong></h4><br>

  <ul>
    <li style = "font-size: 14pt;">Where do you see the rule that decides which songs are included?</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 14pt;">Which part of the code is responsible for creating the new list of songs?</li>
    <li style = "font-size: 14pt;">If you wanted a “Top 5” playlist instead, what is the one place you would change?</li>
  </ul>

  <div style="clear:both;"></div>
</div>


<img src="https://images.code.org/56fe0b2f27b93e2c89457ffa809bcb3a-image-1767640225831.png" alt="JavaScript code sample with a filtering function for a top ten playlist" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

---

## Breaking it down



#### **1. The data (array of objects)** 

<img src="https://images.code.org/30ef70a79fc83fd49505ab06ebacfc9f-image-1767647844111.png" alt="array of songs in javascript" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 20px;">

#### This is the data the app starts with. 
#### Each song is an object, and all the songs together are stored in an array. 

<br><br>


#### **2. The filter logic (inside a function)**

<div style="clear:both;"></div>
<img src="https://images.code.org/f51cf047864f3ebdfb3c48caad10221b-image-1767647898133.png" alt="array of songs in javascript" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 20px;">


#### This function defines the filtering rule.
#### It looks at each song and keeps only the ones with a rank of 10 or less.

<br><br>


#### **3. Using the function**

<img src="https://images.code.org/7241520635924315620d7a77e4e7e3b2-image-1767647866565.png" alt="array of songs in javascript" width="450px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 20px;">

#### This line runs the function using the original data.
#### The result is a new array that contains only the songs that match the rule.

---
## What's next?

#### Let's practice filtering and then learn about the **searching function.**

